Li), zlu@math.uci.edu\n(Z. Lu), zhou_yi@mail.bnu.edu.cn (Y. Zhou).\n1 The ﬁrst author is partially supported by Beijing Natural Science Foundation (Z190003), NSFC (No. \n11522103, 11331002) and by the Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities of China.\n2 The third author is partially supported by the National Science Foundation of USA (DMS-19-08513).\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.laa.2020.01.027\n0024-3795/© 2020 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn some conjectures by Lu and Wenzel - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0024379520300379)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # On some conjectures by Lu and Wenzel ... In particular, we prove this conjecture and hence all conjectures in some special cases. ... In addition, the developed methods give some new simple proofs of the complex BW inequality and its condition for equality.\n\n## Linear Algebra and its Applications\n\nDate: 1 May 2020\n\nPages: 134-164\n\nVolume: Volume 592\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# On some conjectures by Lu and Wenzel\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Jianquan Ge ^{a} ^{1}, Fagui Li ^{a}, Zhiqin Lu ^{b} ^{2}, Yi Zhou ^{a}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issueView Open Manuscript\n\n10.1016/j.laa.2020.01.027\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn order to give a unified generalization of the BW inequality and the DDVV inequality, Lu and Wenzel proposed several conjectures in 2016. In this paper we discuss further these conjectures and put forward a couple of new conjectures which will be shown equivalent to one of the conjectures. In particular, we prove this conjecture and hence all conjectures in some special cases. Another conjecture is shown with a slightly bigger upper bound. In addition, the developed methods give some new simple proofs of the complex BW inequality and its condition for equality.\n\n## MSC\n\n15A45\n\n;\n\n15B57\n\n;\n\n53C42\n\n## Keywords\n\nDDVV-type inequalities\n\n;\n\nBöttcher-Wenzel inequality\n\n;\n\nCommutator\n\nThe first author is partially supported by Beijing Natural Science Foundation (Z190003), NSFC (No. 11522103, 11331002) and by the Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities of China.\n\n^{2}\n    \n\nThe third author is partially supported by the National Science Foundation of USA (DMS-19-08513).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpenProblemsInNLA/matrix-inequalities-and-norms/MI-14/problem.tex at main · ajt60gaibb/OpenProblemsInNLA · GitHub (https://github.com/ajt60gaibb/OpenProblemsInNLA/blob/main/matrix-inequalities-and-norms/MI-14/problem.tex)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; complex Lu--Wenzel spectral conjecture\\par} ... \\textit{Lu-Wenzel conjecture proof 2026},\n\n% Standalone problem document, generated from the adjacent README.md.\n\n% Compile with XeLaTeX. Mathematical content is maintained in Markdown.\n\n\\hypersetup{colorlinks=true,linkcolor=accent,urlcolor=accent,pdftitle={MI-14 - The\n\ncomplex Lu--Wenzel spectral\n\nconjecture},pdfauthor={Open Problems in Numerical Linear Algebra}}\n\n{\\sffamily\\small\\color{muted}Matrix inequalities and norms\\par}\n\n\\vspace{3pt}\n\n{\\raggedright\\hyphenpenalty=10000\\exhyphenpenalty=10000\\sffamily\\bfseries\\fontsize{21}{24}\\selectfont\\color{ink}The\n\ncomplex Lu--Wenzel spectral conjecture\\par}\n\n\\vspace{7pt}\n\n{\\sffamily\\small\\textbf{Difficulty:} extreme\\quad\\textbf{Importance:} interesting\n\nto the community\\par}\n\n{\\sffamily\\small\\bfseries\\color{accent}Status: Partially resolved\\par}\n\n\\vspace{4pt}\n\n{\\color{accent}\\hrule height 0.8pt}\n\n\\vspace{6pt}\n\n\\textbf{Provenance:} explicit conjecture\n\n\\textbf{Rating rationale:} Uniform control of the full commutator\n\nspectrum is a major strengthening of established norm bounds; it\n\nconnects Sylvester operators with geometric matrix inequalities.\n\\tightlist\n\n\\item\n\nJ. Ge, F. Li, Z. Lu, and Y. Zhou, \\emph{On some conjectures by Lu and\n\nWenzel}, arXiv:1908.06624v1, Conjecture 7; Theorems 1.1--1.2 and §4.\n\n\\href{https://arxiv.org/html/1908.06624v1}{Primary manuscript}.\n\n\\item\n\nJ. Ge, F. Li, Z. Tang, and Y. Zhou, \\emph{A survey on the DDVV-type\n\ninequalities}, Advances in Mathematics (China) 53 (2024), 449--467:\n\npublished Conjecture 4.6 and Theorems 4.1--4.2, pp.463--464. These are\n\nConjecture 4.14 and Theorems 4.17--4.18 in\n\n\\href{https://arxiv.org/html/2402.01085v1}{arXiv:2402.01085v1}.\n\n\\href{https://ccj.pku.edu.cn/Article/DownLoad?id=374327987&type=ArticleFile}{Published\n\nPDF}.\n\n\\item\n\nZ. Liu, \\emph{The Lu--Wenzel Spectral Conjecture for Square-Zero\n\nMatrices}, Zenodo record 21693383, abstract.\n\n\\href{https://zenodo.org/records/21693383}{Author's preprint record}.\n\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{Status check --- 2026-09-10}\\label{status-check-2026-09-10}\n\nBoth arXiv sources remain v1. The published survey retains the\n\nconjecture and records the normal, rank-one, and \\(n=2,3\\) cases. Liu's\n\nrecord claims the additional class \\(X^2=0\\) and scalar translates; its\n\nabstract does not claim the unrestricted assertion. That record's\n\ndisplayed publication date and July 2026 creation metadata differ, so no\n\npriority date is inferred from it. Searches used\n\n\\textit{Lu-Wenzel conjecture proof 2026},\n\n\\textit{Lu Wenzel conjecture solved}, and\n\n\\textit{Lu-Wenzel spectral conjecture}. No general resolution was\n\nlocated. Equivalent majorization and fundamental-commutator\n\nformulations, and the real restriction, are grouped in this entry.\n\n\\textbf{Audit update (2026-09-10):} Rechecked the 2024 survey's\n\nConjecture 4.14 and special-case discussion, and searched for later\n\nLu--Wenzel spectral results. The normal, rank-one and low-order cases\n\nare within the displayed target; the square-zero preprint claims only\n\nanother restricted class. This is a bounded literature check, not a\n\nproof that no solution exists.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nREMARKS ON THE B¨OTTCHER-WENZEL INEQUALITY (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=78c693d3eaaea4fb01109506008e764544ef8a87&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; In 2005, B¨ottcher and Wenzel raised the conjecture that if ... The B¨ottcher-Wenzel inequality was proved in [2] for 2 × 2 matrices and ... jecture were obtained by Lu [9], Vong and Jin [10], B¨ottcher-Wenzel [3], and ... The complex matrix case was treated in B¨ottcher-Wenzel [3]\nREMARKS ON THE B¨OTTCHER-WENZEL INEQUALITY\nZHIQIN LU\nAbstract. In 2005, B¨ottcher and Wenzel raised the conjecture that if\nX, Y are real square matrices, then ||XY −Y X||2 ≤2||X||2||Y ||2, where\n||·|| is the Frobenius norm. Various proofs of this conjecture were found\nin the last few years by several authors. We here give another proof. This\nproof is highly conceptual and requires minimal computation. We also\nbrieﬂy discuss related inequalities, in particular, the classical Chern-do\nCamo-Kobayashi inequality.\n1. The proof\nThe B¨ottcher-Wenzel inequality was proved in [2] for 2 × 2 matrices and\nby L´aszl´o [8] for 3×3 matrices. Diﬀerent proofs of the full version of the con-\njecture were obtained by Lu [9], Vong and Jin [10], B¨ottcher-Wenzel [3], and\nAudenaert [1]. The complex matrix case was treated in B¨ottcher-Wenzel [3]\nand Wenzel [11]. A convenient observation that links the complex case to\nthe real one can be found in Cheng-Vong-Wenzel [4, page 296]. A useful\nobservation was obtained in Audenaert [12] for further generalizations.\nIn this section, we give a new proof. Let [X, Y ] denote the commutator\nof X and Y and let ∥· ∥stand for the Frobenius norm (= Hilbert-Schmidt\nnorm).\nTheorem 1. Let X, Y be real n × n matrices. Then\n||[X, Y ]||2 ≤2||X||2 · ||Y ||2.\narXiv:1106.1827v2  [math.RA]  25 Aug 2011\nIn accordance with [9, Lemma 3], we made the following deﬁnition. Let\nV = gl(n, R) and deﬁne a linear map T = TX by\nT : V →V,\nY 7→[XT , [X, Y ]],\nwhere XT is the transpose of X.\nLet\n\n\nΛ =\n\n\n\n\ns1\n...\nsn\nDate: May 31, 2011.\n2000 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. Primary: 15A52, 15A45, 60H25, 65F35 .\nKey words and phrases. Commutator, Matrix inequalities, Best constants.\nThe author is partially supported by NSF award DMS-0904653.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn some conjectures by Lu and Wenzel (https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.06624)\nciteturn2academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.2 years ago; Title: On some conjectures by Lu and Wenzel ... In this paper we discuss further these conjectures and put forward several new conjectures which will be shown equivalent to Conjecture 2. ... In addition, we obtain some new simple proofs of the complex BW inequality and the condition for equality.\nTitle: On some conjectures by Lu and Wenzel\nAuthors: Jianquan Ge, Fagui Li, Zhiqin Lu, Yi Zhou\nDate: Mon Aug 19 07:34:56 2019\n\nIn order to give a unified generalization of the BW inequality and the DDVV inequality, Lu and Wenzel proposed three Conjectures 1, 2, 3 and an open Question 1 in 2016. In this paper we discuss further these conjectures and put forward several new conjectures which will be shown equivalent to Conjecture 2. In particular, we prove Conjecture 2 and hence all conjectures in some special cases. For Conjecture 3, we obtain a bigger upper bound $2+\\sqrt{10}/2$, and we also give a weaker answer for the more general Question 1. In addition, we obtain some new simple proofs of the complex BW inequality and the condition for equality.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA24001.dvi (https://ccj.pku.edu.cn/Article/DownLoad?id=374327987&type=ArticleFile)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Conjecture 4.3 (Lu–Wenzel [58–59]) ... speciﬁcally, for any n × n complex matrix X with ∥X∥= 1, we deﬁne\n462\nADVANCES IN MATHEMATICS (CHINA)\nVol. 53\n4.2 Generalized Peng–Terng 2nd-gap\nBy Theorem 2.5, the quantity S + λ2 might be the right object to study pinching theorems.\nTo justify this, we introduce the following Lu’s conjecture:\nConjecture 4.2 (Lu [56])\nLet M be an n-dimensional closed minimal submanifold in the\nunit sphere Sn+m. If S + λ2 is a constant and if\nS + λ2 > n,\nthen there is a constant ε(n, m) > 0 such that\nS + λ2 > n + ε(n, m).\nIf m = 1, this conjecture is true (cf., [29, 52, 66]). In fact, this is a special case of Chern’s\nconjecture (cf., [18–19]). For more details, please refer to [9, 67, 69, 72], etc.\n4.3 Lu–Wenzel Conjectures\nIn order to give a uniﬁed generalization of the BW inequality, DDVV inequality and Lu\ninequality, Lu–Wenzel[58−59] proposed several conjectures (also called LW Conjectures) in 2016.\nThey started with the following Conjectures 4.3–4.5 and an open Question 4.6 in the space\nM(n, K) of n × n matrices over the ﬁeld K = R.\nConjecture 4.3 (Lu–Wenzel [58–59])\nLet B1, . . . , Bm ∈M(n, K) subject to\ntr(Bα[Bγ, Bβ]) = 0\nfor any 1 ≤α, β, γ ≤m. Then\nm\nX\n m\nX\nα=1\n∥Bα∥2\n!2\n.\nα,β=1\n∥[Bα, Bβ]∥2 ≤\nConjecture 4.4 (Fundamental Conjecture of Lu–Wenzel [58–59])\nLet B, B2, . . . , Bm ∈\nM(n, K) be matrices such that\n(1) tr(BαB∗\nβ) = 0, i.e., Bα⊥Bβ for any α ̸= β;\n \n(2) tr(Bα[B, Bβ]) = 0 for any 2 ≤α, β ≤m.\nThen\nm\nX\nm\nX\n∥B∥2.\nα=2\n∥[B, Bα]∥2 ≤\nα=2\n∥Bα∥2\n!\nmax\n2≤α≤m ∥Bα∥2 +\nNote that the Lu inequality is a special case of Conjecture 4.4.\nLet us consider the linear operator TX as in the following conjectures and questions. More\nspeciﬁcally, for any n × n complex matrix X with ∥X∥= 1, we deﬁne\nTX : M(n, C) −→M(n, C),\nY 7−→[X∗, [X, Y ]].\nIt turns out that TX is exactly an operator on V = M(n, C) and dimC V = n2.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOne-parameter generalization of the Böttcher-Wenzel inequality and its application to open quantum dynamics - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0024379522003470)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Later, László proved the BW inequality for $3\\times 3$ real matrices [11], Lu [12] and, independently, Wong and Jin [14] proved it for $n\\times n$ real matrices, after which the complex case was proved by Böttcher and Wenzel in [3]. ... We conjecture that the theorem is also true for any matrices $A,B\\in {M}_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$.\nLater, László proved the BW inequality for $3\\times 3$ real matrices [11], Lu [12] and, independently, Wong and Jin [14] proved it for $n\\times n$ real matrices, after which the complex case was proved by Böttcher and Wenzel in [3].\n\nOn the other hand, motivated by a problem in the field of open quantum systems [1], we have introduced the following real-valued functional in [4]:$r(A,B)=\\frac{1}{2}(〈[B,A],BA〉+〈[B,{A}^{⁎}],B{A}^{⁎}〉)$ for any $A,B\\in {M}_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$ where {A}^{⁎}${A}^{⁎}$ is the Hermitian conjugation of A and $〈A,B〉=\\mathrm{tr}({A}^{⁎}B)$ is the Hilbert-Schmidt inner product. The sharp bound for the r-function was shown in [4]:$r(A,B)\\le \\frac{1+\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\Vert A\\Vert }^{2}{\\Vert B\\Vert }^{2}.$ This has been used to derive a universal constraint [6], [9], [10] for relaxation rates which is satisfied by any quantum Markovian dynamics described by the completely positive dynamical semigroup, or the GKLS master equation [7]. For this reason, we called the functional (2) the r-function, where “r” stands for relaxation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombinatorics, Probability and Computing (2023), 32, pp. 134–150 (https://sites.miamioh.edu/jiangt/files/2025/01/Many-Turan-exponents.pdf)\nciteturn2search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; The t-blowu